<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aisha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 614–678 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aisha bint Abi Bakr was a wife of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and daughter of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abu Bakr. She was an important transmitter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and a respected religious authority. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a> she opposed the caliph Ali and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a> in 656, part of the civil strife that marked the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aisha was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> c. 614, the daughter of Abu Bakr and Umm Ruman, two of Muhammad's companions. The sources give little more about her childhood. After the death of Muhammad's first wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid">Khadija bint Khuwaylid</a>, his aunt Khawlah bint Hakim proposed that he marry Aisha, and her earlier engagement to Jubayr ibn Mut'im was annulled. The orientalist W. Montgomery Watt suggests that Muhammad hoped to strengthen his ties with Abu Bakr, as marriages in Arabian culture commonly did.</p><p>All extant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a> agree that the marriage took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> but was not consummated until the month of Shawwal after the hijrah to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in April 623. Classical sources give her age as six at marriage and nine at consummation, though Ibn Hisham's biography suggests she may have been ten. Her age later became a subject of modern debate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition Aisha ranks second only to Khadija among Muhammad's wives in his affection. Muhammad died in her apartment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. She remained active in the Muslim community for 44 years afterwards and is credited with narrating over 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>, on ritual, inheritance, pilgrimage and other subjects. She issued legal opinions, and companions such as Urwah ibn al-Zubayr consulted her. She was known by the title al-Ṣiddīqa bint al-Ṣiddīq, &quot;the truthful woman, daughter of the truthful man&quot;.</p><p>In 656 the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Uthman, was killed by rebels, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>. Aisha, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talha-ibn-ubayd-allah">Talha ibn Ubayd Allah</a> and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, mobilised forces against the new caliph, Ali, demanding punishment for those responsible. The two sides met near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> on which Aisha directed her troops from a howdah. Her forces were defeated and about 10,000 Muslims were killed, in what is considered the first major battle between Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ali reproached Aisha but pardoned her and sent her back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> under an escort led by her brother, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr. She withdrew from public politics and became a teacher, and in her last two years she spent much time telling stories of Muhammad. She died at her home in Medina on 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> 58 AH (16 July 678). Abu Hurayra led her funeral prayer, and she was buried in the al-Baqi cemetery.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> reveres her as a leading scholar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> transmitter and teacher of several companions and of the tabi'in. Shia Muslims generally view her critically because of her opposition to Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aisha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ali’s caliphate was consumed by war against <b>Aisha</b>, the Prophet’s widow, and her allies Talha and al-Zubayr at the Battle of the Camel in 656 CE, and against Muawiya at the inconclusive Battle of Siffin in 657 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Quran gave women rights of inheritance and property, and women such as Khadija and <b>Aisha</b> were central figures of early Islam; customs such as veiling and seclusion took many forms, and in modern times women’s rights and roles have been among the most debated questions in Muslim…</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=655" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aisha"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5778 5697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5778 5697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=655">What was here in 655 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
